{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WebGW9FMJENyCshGZajWRX","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/paper/experiments.tex:107:The \\texttt{exception\\_only} format includes a \\texttt{[No exception data in trace]} marker in all pass-class samples (regex baseline B5 exploits this for 99.33\\% accuracy; Appendix~\\ref{sec:regex_baselines}).\ndocs/paper/discussion.tex:26:The gap between B5 (format marker, 99.33\\%) and SFT \\texttt{exception\\_only} (99.83\\%) isolates the value of learned semantic understanding (McNemar $p{=}0.041$, Table~\\ref{tab:regex}).\ndocs/paper/discussion.tex:39:The B5 baseline (Appendix~\\ref{sec:regex_baselines}) achieves 99.33\\% by exploiting a deterministic format marker (\\texttt{[No exception data in trace]}) present in all pass samples.\ndocs/paper/main.aux:1555:\\newlabel{tab:regex}{{15}{17}{Regex baseline accuracy ($N{=}1{,}190$). B4 is the strongest semantic baseline (6.22pp below SFT \\texttt {exception\\_only}, McNemar $p{<}0.001$). B6 uses an error count threshold. $^\\ddag $B5 exploits a data formatting artifact: the \\texttt {[No exception data in trace]} marker is deterministically present in all pass samples, making this a format-level shortcut rather than a genuine exception-based heuristic}{table.caption.48}{}}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01M2jYRWzyTk7S9Gq1VdAqUk","session_id":"1de37377-3b2d-4064-93c3-e74486d99dc5","uuid":"e9a65297-e26a-417c-9389-a38e4554e04a","timestamp":"2026-05-18T14:11:15.166Z"}
